module

public import StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.InvertibleSummandNoSliceSheafify
public import StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.InvertibleSummandSheafify
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.CoherentQuasicoherence
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.FiniteLocallyFreeCoherent

/-!
# Invertible modules on a locally Noetherian algebraic space are coherent

Let `F` be an algebraic space over a scheme `S` contained in a big fppf site `B`, and let `M` be
an `𝒪_F`-module on the small étale site of `F` which is **invertible**
(`Stacks.SheafOfModules.IsInvertible`, Stacks 01CS): tensoring by `M` is an equivalence of the
category of `𝒪_F`-modules.  If `F` is locally Noetherian then `M` is coherent.  This is one of
the three particular conclusions of Stacks 07UB.

**The route does not pass through finite local freeness, and it cannot.**  An invertible module
on a general site need *not* be finite locally free: Stacks 0B8M says so in its parenthesis, and
exhibits the counterexample — a one-point space whose ring of functions `R` has nontrivial
Picard group, where the invertible modules are the invertible `R`-modules while the finite
locally free ones are the finite free ones.  The converse direction of Stacks 0B8M needs the
stalks of the structure sheaf to be local rings, which is not information the small étale site
carries in this tree.  What *is* available on an arbitrary site is Stacks 0B8N (b), proved as
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.exists_cover_isDirectSummand_finiteFree`: on a covering of any object an
invertible module is a **split direct summand** of a finite free module.  Being a split summand
of a finite free module is strictly weaker than being finite free, and it is exactly enough for
coherence, because every input the coherence argument of
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.FiniteLocallyFreeCoherent` takes from a trivialization is
a *retract* statement about sections.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.IsRetractOfFiniteFree`: a sheaf of modules whose sections are, naturally
  in the object of the site, a split direct summand of the finitely supported families of
  sections of the structure sheaf.  This is the sectionwise reading of "a split subobject of a
  finite free module", at an arbitrary module universe.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsNoetherianAffineRetractChart`: an étale chart which is affine with
  Noetherian ring of sections and over whose slice `M` is a finite free retract.

## Main results

* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.isRetractOfFiniteFree_over_of_le`: being a finite free retract on a slice
  site is inherited by every smaller slice site.  Unlike its finite-free analogue
  `Stacks.SheafOfModules.isFiniteFree_over_of_le` this needs no additivity and no preservation
  of coproducts: the sectionwise data is simply reindexed along `CategoryTheory.Over.map`.
* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.isRetractOfFiniteFree_of_isSplitMono_cofan` and
  `Stacks.SheafOfModules.isRetractOfFiniteFree_of_isSplitMono_pi`: a split subobject of a finite
  free module is a finite free retract, the second for the finite *product* spelling in which
  Stacks 0B8N (b) delivers its conclusion.
* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.exists_generates_of_isRetractOfFiniteFree_over` and
  `Stacks.SheafOfModules.isFiniteType_of_cover_isRetractOfFiniteFree`: a finite free retract over
  a chart is generated by finitely many of its sections over that chart, and a module which is
  locally a finite free retract is of finite type in the sectionwise sense of Stacks 03DL(5).
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.bijective_sectionsBaseChange_of_finsuppRetract` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.bijective_sectionsBaseChange_of_isRetractOfFiniteFree_over`: **the base
  change comparison of `M` over any morphism of charts lying over a chart on which `M` is a
  finite free retract is bijective.**  No affineness and no cohomological input.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_covering_through_noetherianAffineRetract`: every étale chart of
  a locally Noetherian algebraic space is covered by charts which are affine, have Noetherian
  ring of sections, and over which an invertible `M` is a finite free retract.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isCoherentOnSite_of_isInvertible`,
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isCoherent_of_isInvertible` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isCoherent_of_isInvertible_instance`: **an invertible module on a
  locally Noetherian algebraic space is coherent**, as the site statement, as a theorem and as
  an instance.

## Implementation notes

**Why the retract is recorded sectionwise.**  `Stacks.SheafOfModules.IsRetractOfFiniteFree` could
equally be stated as the existence of a split monomorphism into a finite coproduct of copies of
the structure sheaf.  It is not, for two reasons.  First, both consumers —
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.bijective_sectionsBaseChange_of_finsuppRetract`, which is a computation
with a tensor product of sections, and
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.exists_generates_of_isRetractOfFiniteFree_over`, which is a computation with
restriction maps — read the data on sections and nothing else.  Second, the sectionwise form
restricts to a smaller slice site by reindexing alone, whereas the sheaf-level form has to be
pushed along `SheafOfModules.overMap` and recombined with
`SheafOfModules.overFunctorMap`.  The predicate is therefore the `SheafOfModules.IsFiniteFree`
analogue of a retract, and shares that predicate's freedom in the module universe: the
finitely supported families live in the universe of the structure sheaf while the module's
sections need not.

**The spellings already in the tree, and why none of them is reused.**  "A split direct summand
of a finite free sheaf of modules" is written out repeatedly — nine times, in nine files — and
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.IsRetractOfFiniteFree` is not a tenth copy of any of them.  The four
nearest are taken one at a time below.  The other five need no discussion:
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.IsLocallyFiniteFreeSummand` of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Ample/LocallyFiniteFreeSummand.lean` — in this same namespace —
`StrictlyPerfect.IsDirectSummandOfFiniteFree` of
`StacksFrontier/Topology/Sheaves/Cohomology/StrictlyPerfect/FiniteFreeSummands.lean`,
`Stacks09U6.IsDirectSummandOfFiniteFree` of
`StacksFrontier/Topology/Sheaves/Cohomology/StrictlyPerfectRestriction/PullbackOverASite.lean` and
an inline `Nonempty (Retract …)` in the formalization of Tag 08C7 are all stated for a ringed
*space*; and `IsFiniteFreeSummand` of
`StacksFrontier/Topology/Sheaves/Cohomology/StrictlyPerfect/TensorOfFiniteFreeSummands.lean`,
the most general of
the nine — an arbitrary preadditive monoidal category with finite biproducts and a zero object —
asks for a `CategoryTheory.MonoidalCategory` structure on the `𝒪_F`-modules, which instance
search does not supply at these universes, though it does supply the preadditivity, the finite
biproducts and the zero object.
`IsDirectSummandOfFiniteFree` of
`StacksFrontier/CategoryTheory/Sites/Cohomology/StrictlyPerfect/ConeOfAMorphism.lean` is written
with `SheafOfModules.free (Fin n)`, which needs `HasWeakSheafify` and `WEqualsLocallyBijective` for
the topology, and is monomorphic at ring and module universe `0`; the Tag 0FNZ statements of
`StacksFrontier/Algebra/Category/ModuleCat/Sheaf/LocalFiniteFreeSummand.lean` spell the same
condition inline as `Nonempty (Retract (F.over V) (SheafOfModules.free …))` and need those two
instances on *every* slice.  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.IsDirectSummandOfFiniteFree` of
`StacksFrontier/Topology/Sheaves/Cohomology/StrictlyPerfect/FiniteFreeSummands.lean` is the
finite-product spelling at a free universe, but it carries no slice propagation at all — none of the
thirty-five declarations of that file restricts it along `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.over`, which
is the one thing the covering argument below needs of it — and it forces the module universe to
equal the ring universe.  The localization is not itself the obstacle:
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.over` exists, and `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.over_O` identifies the
structure sheaf of the slice with the restriction of the original on the nose.  The private
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.IsFiniteFreeRetract` of
`StacksFrontier/CategoryTheory/Sites/Cohomology/StrictlyPerfect/LocalNullHomotopy.lean` is the cofan
spelling introduced for exactly the slice-propagation reason recorded here, but it too is
monomorphic at universe `0`
and it is private.  The predicate below is the sectionwise form at free ring and module
universes, indexed by a bare sheaf of rings.  Three bridges keep it from becoming a parallel
notion: `Stacks.SheafOfModules.isRetractOfFiniteFree_of_isFiniteFree` from the tree's primary
`SheafOfModules.IsFiniteFree`, and
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.isRetractOfFiniteFree_of_isSplitMono_pi` together with
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.isRetractOfFiniteFree_of_retract_pi` from the finite-product spelling,
the second taking exactly the `CategoryTheory.Retract` datum that
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.IsDirectSummandOfFiniteFree` carries.

**Where the module universe is pinned, and by what.**  Everything up to and including
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsNoetherianAffineRetractChart` is at a free module universe, except the
three bridges out of the finite-product spelling —
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.isRetractOfFiniteFree_of_isSplitMono_cofan`,
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.isRetractOfFiniteFree_of_isSplitMono_pi` and
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.isRetractOfFiniteFree_of_retract_pi` — which are at module universe equal
to the ring universe, because `SheafOfModules.unit` has its sections in the universe of the
structure sheaf.  From `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_cover_isRetractOfFiniteFree_of_isInvertible`
onwards, `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isCoherentOnSite_of_isInvertible` and the instance included, the
module universe is the universe of the structure sheaf, and the hypothesis of invertibility is what
pins it: `Stacks.SheafOfModules.IsInvertible` is defined through the tensor product of sheaves of
modules, which exists only when the module sections share the universe of the structure sheaf, so
an invertible module on the small étale site of an algebraic space over `Scheme.{u}` is a
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.Modules.{u, u}`.  That is the universe in which Stacks 07UB's
invertibility conclusion is stated here.  The neighbouring instance
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isCoherent_of_fact_isFiniteLocallyFreeOnSite` of
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.FiniteLocallyFreeCoherent` is at `Modules.{u, m}`, so of
the two conclusions this is the less general, and unavoidably so.

**Quasi-coherence is not proved separately.**  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsCoherent` is
quasi-coherence together with coherence on the site, and the first follows from the second at
this module universe by `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isCoherent_of_isCoherentOnSite`; so no global
presentation of an invertible module is constructed anywhere below.  The one such construction
that would be needed — the cokernel presentation `P → P → M → 0` coming from the split
idempotent — is avoided for that reason.

**Descent, Proposition 03DX(2) is not used**, exactly as in
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.FiniteLocallyFreeCoherent`: the relations clause of
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isCoherentOnSite_of_isCartesian` asks for the base change property only
between the charts of the covering it is handed, and
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.bijective_sectionsBaseChange_of_isRetractOfFiniteFree_over` supplies it
there.

**The tag.**  Stacks 07UB has five numbered equivalent conditions and three further assertions.
This file proves one of the three, for an invertible module, so
`@[stacks 07UB "invertible modules"]` sits on
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isCoherent_of_isInvertible_instance`, beside
`@[stacks 07UB "structure sheaf"]` and `@[stacks 07UB "finite locally free modules"]`; the bare
tag is not claimed, the five numbered conditions not being proved here.

